+# Tool input validation
+
+| Field  | Value                |
+| ------ | -------------------- |
+| Issue  | re-cinq/HALEngine#37 |
+| Status | Draft                |
+
+The registry owns validation of model-supplied tool input against the same JSON Schema exposed to providers.
+
+## Contract
+
+- Before invoking an executor, `execute` rejects missing required properties, wrong declared types and invalid enum members with a readable `ToolResponse.result` naming the property path and constraint; neither returned diagnostics nor logs disclose input values, while valid input (including unknown keys) reaches the executor unchanged exactly once, without coercion or defaults, and unknown tool names still throw `Unknown tool: <name>`. ([validated by: rejects malformed input privately and passes valid input unchanged](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L27))
+- Static schemas compile at registration, fail there with the tool name if uncompilable, and are reused over ten executions. ([validated by: compiles static schemas at registration and reuses them](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L86))
+- Function-valued definitions use the schema resolved for the current execution and cache compilation by schema object identity. ([validated by: validates the current dynamic schema and caches each schema object](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L108))
+
+## Delivery
+
+Use a runtime JSON Schema validator dependency, with no opt-out, coercion, defaults or implicit ban on extra properties. Input rejections are tool results available to the model for correction within the existing five-round limit; unknown tool names retain their exception contract. The one-line `execute` doc comment points here.
+
+Update README.md, docs/adding-a-tool.md and .specify/spec.md to explain this contract, add an npm-facing changelog entry, and make a MINOR version bump. The migration note must explain that executors no longer receive malformed input to produce their own error messages. Build/import, type checks, lint, formatting and the existing tool-loop tests remain CI obligations.
